    <h2>The Shoals Runs on a Reliable Handyman</h2>

    <p>The handyman fills the gap between "too small for a specialized contractor" and "more than I can do myself," and in a region of aging housing that gap is enormous. A loose railing, a sticking door, a run of rotten trim, a ceiling fan that needs hanging, a bathroom caulk-and-refresh — none of it justifies a dedicated tradesman, all of it needs doing. Across the four Shoals towns, that adds up to a steady, unglamorous stream of work for someone dependable. The demand isn't the question here; the reliable supply is.</p>

    <h2>Old-Home Punch Lists and New-Home To-Dos</h2>

    <p>The work changes character with the housing. In the older <a href="/locations/florence/">Florence</a> and <a href="/locations/sheffield/">Sheffield</a> homes, it's a long tail of small repairs the years pile up — the settled door that won't latch, the trim pulling loose, the dozen tiny fixes a hundred-year-old house always has waiting. In the newer Muscle Shoals subdivisions, it's more often installs and setup for people who just moved in: TV mounts, shelving, blinds, the punch list a new house accumulates as a family makes it theirs. A handyman working the whole region shifts between nursing old houses and outfitting new ones, sometimes in the same week.</p>

    <h2>Where the Handyman Line Actually Is</h2>

    <p>The most important thing a good handyman knows is what not to touch. Small carpentry, patching, mounting, fixture swaps, and general repair are fair game. Work inside an electrical panel, new circuits, gas lines, and structural or larger plumbing work belong to the licensed trades, and the honest handyman refers those out instead of playing electrician for an afternoon. That boundary protects the homeowner and the handyman both. What goes wrong is the unlicensed generalist who takes on a panel or a gas job to keep the money in-house — that's exactly the work where a mistake is dangerous, and it's a red flag when a handyman offers it. Knowing the limit is a feature, not a shortcoming.</p>

    <h2 id="pricing-summary">What Handyman Work Costs in the Shoals</h2>

    <p>Real ranges, and what moves them:</p>
    <ul class="trades-list-block">
      <li><strong>Hourly rate</strong> — about $60 to $90 an hour, often with a one- or two-hour minimum.</li>
      <li><strong>Half-day of work</strong> — roughly $250 to $400 plus materials.</li>
      <li><strong>Full day</strong> — around $450 to $700 plus materials.</li>
      <li><strong>Flat-rate small jobs</strong> — priced per task for common, predictable work.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The trip and setup are much of the cost, so batching a punch list of several items into one visit is the efficient way to hire — eight small fixes in one trip beats eight separate service calls.</p>

    <h2>Covering Both Sides of the River</h2>

    <p>The handyman's advantage is reach. A single dependable person can work Lauderdale County on the north side — Florence and out toward Killen, Rogersville, and Anderson — and Colbert County on the south, from Muscle Shoals and Sheffield to the Cherokee and Leighton satellites. That cross-river coverage is hard for a homeowner to find, because most small-job operators stick to one side. The handyman who reliably crosses the Tennessee River in both directions taps a market twice the size, and word travels fast among neighbors who've finally found someone who answers.</p>

